  "dataCard": {},
  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/rineloricaria_lanceolata_en/",
      "html": "<p>Rineloricaria lanceolata is a whiptail catfish that is very variable in coloration. Almost all colours from deep dark brown (almost black) to a light clay-brown can be observed.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/rineloricaria_lanceolata_en/",
      "html": "<p>Recent wild-collected imports of this species come from Paraguay.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/rineloricaria_lanceolata_en/",
      "html": "<p>Rineloricaria lanceolata is somewhat feared by breeders, because many males are bad fathers that prefer to eat the spawn.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/10-catfishes/10b-catfishes-suckermouths-plecos-and-l-numbers/rineloricaria_lanceolata_en/",
      "html": "<p>In the 1980s a brick-red sport of whiptail catfish appeared in the former GDR. The real origin of the fish remains a mystery, but it was possible to cross it with R. lanceolata. The strains that exist nowadays are most probably all hybrids. The initial red whiptail catfish most probably belonged to another species than R. lanceolata (see Evers &amp; Seidel, Wels-Atlas, Vol. 1: 694, for details).</p><p>Among the recently imported wild-collected fish is one red male. It was given, along with some conspecifics of the same importation, to a breeder, and it remains to be seen whether another, pure-bred, red strain can be established.</p>"
    }
  ],
